new video shows the dramatic rescue of  survivors from a plane crash in Alaska  the Coast Guard released this footage  overnight it shows a rescue helicopter  hovering 100 feet above the rocky crash  site the 11 survivors were hoisted off  mount jumbo on Prince of Wales Island  where it went down on the way to catch a  can Kris Van Cleave spoke to the Coast  Guard crew members behind the operation  Kris good morning good morning  investigators say one thing is clear the  people on that plane are very lucky it's  increasingly looking like the plane  essentially flew into the side of a  mountain the pilot told the NTSB by the  time he realized he was in trouble he  was out of time the camera on the Coast  Guard helicopters hoist shows the daring  rescue operation under deteriorating  conditions hovering just 100 feet above  the crashed plane on Alaska's mount  jumbo to get everyone out the chopper  was filled to capacity  survivors congratulated each other  inside after cheating death pilot Joe  Plunkett a rescue swimmer Tony Puglia  say their crew braved bad weather and  dwindling fuel to find the wreckage  flying as close as 50 feet from the side  of the mountain through near blinding  fog at times unable to see the ground  they talked to CBS News via FaceTime  from a remote Alaska airstrip the  injuries weren't like incredibly severe  and there were fatalities the pilot of  this two Havilland Otter 72 year old  Mike Hudgens told investigators his  flight plan went through mountainous  terrain after taking off he ran into  clouds at about 1,100 feet with  visibility decreasing and as the weather  got worse he essentially became  disoriented to the plane's position when  he saw the mountains rising around the  plane he tried to climb but it was too  late  passenger ginny hacker iseman thanked  the Coast Guard crew on Facebook those  brave men put their lives on the line  for us I know that we would have never  lasted the night up there about five  minutes to get out of there we were very  lucky that's remarkable  you guys  worried we were sweating for the whole  time at last report two of the eleven  people on board remain hospitalized they  are expected to survive the company that  owns the plane praised Hudgens as a  skilled professional with thousands of  hours of flying experience in Alaska  Alex what a dramatic story thanks Chris
 

